question.:question/problem:
Find all real values of $k$ for which the vectors $\mathbf{a}=\langle 3,1,4\rangle$, $\mathbf{b}=\langle 1,-1,-1\rangle$, and $\mathbf{c}=\langle 0,-2,- k^{2}\rangle$ are coplanar.

**In ascending order**, enter both exact values.

$k=${{field-1}} and $k=${{field-2}}

question.:question/worked-solution:
The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}3&1&4\\1&-1&-1\\0&-2&- k^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&=(3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-1&-1\\-2&- k^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(1)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\0&- k^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(4)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\0&-2\end{vmatrix}\\&=(3)\cdot ((-1)\cdot (- k^{2})-(-1)\cdot (-2))\\&\quad -(1)\cdot ((1)\cdot (- k^{2})-(-1)\cdot (0))\\&\quad +(4)\cdot ((1)\cdot (-2)-(-1)\cdot (0))\\&=(3)\cdot (k^{2} - 2)\\&\quad -(1)\cdot (- k^{2})\\&\quad +(4)\cdot (-2)\\&=4 k^{2} - 14
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
4 k^{2} - 14&=0\\4 k^{2}&=14\\k^{2}&=\frac{7}{2}\\k&=\pm \sqrt{\frac{7}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Both signs are necessary because both have square $\frac{7}{2}$. Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=-\sqrt{\frac{7}{2}}$ or $k=\sqrt{\frac{7}{2}}$.

question.:question/problem:
Find all real values of $k$ for which the vectors $\mathbf{a}=\langle -3,-4,3\rangle$, $\mathbf{b}=\langle -4,4,4\rangle$, and $\mathbf{c}=\langle 1,0,- k^{2}\rangle$ are coplanar.

**In ascending order**, enter both exact values.

$k=${{field-1}} and $k=${{field-2}}

question.:question/worked-solution:
The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}-3&-4&3\\-4&4&4\\1&0&- k^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}4&4\\0&- k^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(-4)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-4&4\\1&- k^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-4&4\\1&0\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-3)\cdot ((4)\cdot (- k^{2})-(4)\cdot (0))\\&\quad -(-4)\cdot ((-4)\cdot (- k^{2})-(4)\cdot (1))\\&\quad +(3)\cdot ((-4)\cdot (0)-(4)\cdot (1))\\&=(-3)\cdot (- 4 k^{2})\\&\quad -(-4)\cdot (4 k^{2} - 4)\\&\quad +(3)\cdot (-4)\\&=28 k^{2} - 28
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
28 k^{2} - 28&=0\\28 k^{2}&=28\\k^{2}&=1\\k&=\pm 1
\end{aligned}
$$

Both signs are necessary because both have square $1$. Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=-1$ or $k=1$.

question.:question/problem:
Find all real values of $k$ for which the vectors $\mathbf{a}=\langle - k^{2},-2,-4\rangle$, $\mathbf{b}=\langle -1,-3,0\rangle$, and $\mathbf{c}=\langle 0,0,4\rangle$ are coplanar.

**In ascending order**, enter both exact values.

$k=${{field-1}} and $k=${{field-2}}

question.:question/worked-solution:
The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}- k^{2}&-2&-4\\-1&-3&0\\0&0&4\end{vmatrix}\\&=(- k^{2})\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-3&0\\0&4\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(-2)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-1&0\\0&4\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(-4)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-1&-3\\0&0\end{vmatrix}\\&=(- k^{2})\cdot ((-3)\cdot (4)-(0)\cdot (0))\\&\quad -(-2)\cdot ((-1)\cdot (4)-(0)\cdot (0))\\&\quad +(-4)\cdot ((-1)\cdot (0)-(-3)\cdot (0))\\&=(- k^{2})\cdot (-12)\\&\quad -(-2)\cdot (-4)\\&\quad +(-4)\cdot (0)\\&=12 k^{2} - 8
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
12 k^{2} - 8&=0\\12 k^{2}&=8\\k^{2}&=\frac{2}{3}\\k&=\pm \sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Both signs are necessary because both have square $\frac{2}{3}$. Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=-\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}$ or $k=\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}$.
